			<div class="content" id="post-content-4208204">
									

<p>Alright, so I'm not sure if it's weird to do a point-by-point response to a point-by-point critique, but&#8230;</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>I agree about the &quot;hitherto&quot; thing. Rereading the article again myself today, I found myself thinking &quot;wow, why did I use <em>that</em> word?&quot;</li>
<li>Good call about this sentence. I've had some Discourse regarding the &quot;appear to be poorly coordinated&quot; clause (my argument is that for all we know, it is fully intending on moving the way it does), but I went ahead and trimmed the entire thing down.</li>
<li>Re: the scare quotes, I added them to acknowledge the possibility that the Stepdad was never &quot;stuck&quot; to begin with, it just appeared to be from our perspective. Perhaps there's a better way to convey this, however, or perhaps it would be redundant to convey it at all.</li>
<li>I removed the &quot;somewhat coherent&quot; part, however I kept &quot;<em>appears</em> to be sapient&quot;, because&#8230;the Foundation can't actually tell whether it IS sapient, or merely mimics the <em>appearance</em> of being sapient. I think that's a big part of the draw of this skip—the &quot;uncanny valley&quot; uneasiness of something that <em>might</em> be a &quot;being&quot;, but might only be <em>approximating</em> one. While it does respond to its surroundings, it doesn't do so in <em>exactly</em> the right way, so the question of whether it actually <em>is</em> sapient is still up for debate.</li>
<li>&quot;Report the disappearance&quot; is way better than what I had. Changed.</li>
<li>Good call on the weird sentence structure. Changed.</li>
<li>I don't think she'd immediately think her son was kidnapped upon merely <em>hearing</em> that he was talking about a stepdad he didn't have. Little kids say completely ridiculous things sometimes, and so I think she'd merely feel <em>worried</em> that something might happen to him until discovering that something <em>DID</em> happen to him.</li>
<li>The teacher didn't give details because the teacher didn't see the Stepdad either, she only heard Johnathan talking about him.</li>
<li>That obvious, huh? Rounded up to 150 kph.</li>
<li>Changed &quot;FBI&quot; to &quot;FBI agent&quot;. Think I've maybe watched too many detective shows.</li>
<li>Changed &quot;bobble around&quot; to &quot;rapidly jitter&quot;. Sounds somewhat more clinical, and feels a bit creepier. I saw a let's play of a late 90s computer game involving extremely bad mocap recently which caused the heads of several characters to twitch and jitter, so that's what inspired me to have this skip do the same thing.</li>
<li>Moved the mention of being &quot;uninjured&quot; to earlier in the transcript.</li>
<li>&quot;Engaging in its usual behavior&quot; is good.</li>
<li>Good call. Don't know <em>what</em> I was thinking with the pillow.</li>
<li>Tough but fair call on the &quot;punchline&quot; bit. It's a difficult line to tread, making an SCP that's amusing in a creepy/surreal way without going full lolfoundation. Removed it.</li>
<li>Regarding the redactions, I censored dates because I'd seen other skips with censored dates, but if you don't think it's necessary I'll uncensor them. I'll also try to think of a name for the hapless researcher in Incident 4092-1. I was thinking that Mrs. W and Officer J's names would be censored though, as they aren't Foundation.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
