			<div class="content" id="post-content-4247974">
									

<p>Your critique is fair. I was in Advanced English Classes in high school so I'm sort of used to meeting a Word Count. I think <em>much</em> of what you're saying is worth editing.</p>
<p>However, I use &quot;resembles&quot; and &quot;appears to be&quot; for a very specific purpose. The Rubber Stepdad isn't a white male, it isn't even human. It is something SHAPED like a human, and PRETENDING (poorly) to be a human, yet extremely alien to human-ness. Likewise, it &quot;appears to be&quot; sapient because, while it does respond to its surroundings, it doesn't respond in <em>exactly</em> the right way. I've tried to make it so that it's very much unknown whether it IS a &quot;being&quot;, or something EMULATING being-ness. Perhaps my allusions to that fact aren't quite clear enough, though. I'm always open to suggestions. :)</p>

